The fix replaces the saturated closed form and makes the Hill step implicit whenever it is stiff. All changes are in pushApartHill, engine.js:179-224.

(1) Replace the saturated closed form at :187-198 with a clamp on F0. Results converge as F0→∞ with error of order inertialScale/F0, and the clamp keeps the momentum exchange through RA and RB that the closed form drops:
  const F0 = Math.min(o.F, 1e9 * inertialScale);

(2) Set stalled from the force balance, as comic mode does:
  if (!(aFree && F0 > RA) && !(bFree && F0 > RB)) return { t: Infinity, stalled: true, saturated: false, xA: 0, xB: 0, vA: 0, vB: 0, pA: 0, pB: 0, EA: 0, EB: 0 };
After this check, the loop result always has stalled = false.

(3) Keep dt = tEst/4000, but take the Hill force implicitly (backward Euler in the relative speed) when a step is stiff:
  const mMove = Math.min(aFree ? mA : Infinity, bFree ? mB : Infinity);
  const tau0 = mMove * vmax / ((1 + 1 / k) * F0);
  const stiff = dt > 0.25 * tau0;
  const after = (wn) => {
    const F = F0 * hill(wn / vmax, k);
    return [
      aFree && (vA > 0 || F > RA) ? Math.max(0, vA + dt * (F - RA) / mA) : 0,
      bFree && (vB > 0 || F > RB) ? Math.max(0, vB + dt * (F - RB) / mB) : 0,
    ];
  };

In the loop:
  if (stiff) {
    let lo = 0, hi = Math.max(vmax, vA + vB);
    for (let i = 0; i < 60; i++) {
      const m = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
      const [a, b] = after(m);
      if (a + b > m) lo = m; else hi = m;
    }
    [nvA, nvB] = after(lo);
  } else {
    // existing explicit midpoint step, unchanged
  }

Why this works:
- sum(after(w)) − w is strictly decreasing, so the bisection always finds the single root.
- Backward Euler is L-stable, so the speed can never overshoot vmax and stall.
- The non-stiff path is unchanged, so calibrateComic() keeps the same comic fractions.

